 bitcoin equals one bitcoin  but if you want to actually take your  bitcoin and spend it for goods or  services or you want to exchange it for  dollars there is volatility we have this  quote right here from noelle atchison  from genesis global in which he states  bitcoin is a volatile asset and in times  of uncertainty harnessing that  volatility which is usually a feature  not a bug is difficult enough to  dissuade even the most experienced  volatility traders this is especially  acute in the current market environment  given that the uncertainty is driven  largely by the war in europe and it is  hard to predict outcomes when we do not  know if the news emerging from the  conflict zone is trustworthy and so  ultimately what we're watching is  certain people like volatility and  others don't if you have a long time  horizon the volatility ends up working  in your favor the fixed supply asset and  as demand increases the price goes up  but if you're a short-term optimized  person or a trader that volatility can  definitely go against you so  understanding who you are what your  goals are what you're optimizing for end  up being really important when you're  looking at bitcoin then if we take a  look at a third criteria as to people  wanting to evaluate bitcoin there's this  idea of lost their stolen funds and  they're worried that if they actually  hold bitcoin somebody will just take it  from them well that's not necessarily  true because there are tons of different  services and exchanges where you can  purchase and store bitcoin you can go  ahead and use those services to either  buy the bitcoin and take it off of the  exchange this idea of not your keys not  your coin you can't exactly do that at  size and scale in the legacy financial  system but you also have to have great  hygiene when it comes to security you  have to remember your seed phrase the  equivalent of a password and you also  have to make sure that you follow best  practices you don't tell people 